sified as surface if the cumulative projection area falls below a threshold. The normal \mathbf{n}i is computed as 	ext{normalize}(-
abla{\mathbf{p}i} C{iho}). Neighboring surface particles are triangulated on the plane perpendicular to \mathbf{n}i, and the area constraint C{iA} = \sum{t \in T(i)}  rac{1}{2} \|(\mathbf{p}{t2} - \mathbf{p}{t1}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t3} - \mathbf{p}{t1})\| is enforced using its Jacobians with respect to triangle vertices. A distance constraint C{ij}^D = \min\{0, \|\mathbf{p}i - \mathbf{p}j\| - d0\} is also applied to promote uniform particle distribution. Surface particles are updated every two time steps to optimize performance. Time Complexity: O(N \cdot k) per iteration, where N is the number of particles and k is the average number of neighbors. With I iterations, total time is O(I \cdot N \cdot k). Spatial hashing reduces neighbor search to O(N) on average. Memory Complexity: O(N) for particle data and neighbor lists. Measured Performance: PBF runs in real-time on modern GPUs for millions of particles. Scaling: Scales well with GPU parallelization due to independent particle updates and neighbor searches. Strengths: Stability for incompressible fluids, easy handling of surface tension, no need for pressure solvers, real-time performance. When to Use: Real-time fluid simulation, visual effects, interactive applications where stability and speed are prioritized. When Not to Use: Scientific simulations requiring high-fidelity compressible flow dynamics or complex non-Newtonian fluid behavior. Position Based Fluids (PBF) is based on Position-Based Dynamics (PBD), which was introduced by Müller et al. in 2007. PBF specifically applies PBD to fluid simulation, enforcing incompressibility via density constraints. It has been widely adopted in computer graphics for real-time fluid effects due to its stability and efficiency. Recent developments include coupling PBF with Gaussian Splatting for rendering (Gaussian Splashing). ShengzhiWu/taichi-volume-renderer, https://github.com/ShengzhiWu/taichi-volume-renderer, fetched 2026-10-26 (unofficial) Gaussian Splashing: Unified